※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A `&mut` copied into a second name is a reborrow.
※
※ **The copy borrows from the name it was copied from**, as `&mut x` borrows from
※ `x`: while it lives the first may not be used, and when its scope ends the first
※ is free again.  Handed to a call it is lent for the call and no longer; read
※ through with `⌖` it is not copied at all.  **A `&` copied from it is a shared
※ reborrow**, beside which the first may still be read.  And a place lent to a
※ name in an inner scope is free again once that scope ends.
※
※ Compare **Rust**'s implicit reborrow, `let other: &mut u8 = r` being
※ `&mut *r` -- with the end at the scope, as Rust had it before non-lexical
※ lifetimes.

let std := ⎕import("std")

@[impure]
fn bump(p: &mut u8):
    p⌖ ← p⌖ + 1u8

fn read(p: &u8) → u8:
    p⌖

@[startup, impure]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    let n: mut u8 = 1u8
    let r: &mut u8 = &mut n
    bump(r)
    bump(r)
    r⌖ ← r⌖ + 1u8
    if #init.args > 0:
        let other: &mut u8 = r
        other⌖ ← other⌖ + 1u8
    r⌖ ← r⌖ + 1u8
    let ro: &u8 = r
    let seen: u8 = read(ro) + r⌖
    let m: mut u8 = 1u8
    if #init.args > 0:
        let inner: &mut u8 = &mut m
        inner⌖ ← 2u8
    m ← m + 1u8
    if seen = 12u8 ∧ m = 3u8: 0u6 else: 1u6
